---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import data from '../../data/editorial-data.json';
import '../../styles/editorial.css';
const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Annotating the global temperature record',
    datePublished: '2026-09-09',
    dateModified: '2026-09-09',
    author: { '@id': 'https://www.chartsai.com/#organization' },
  },
  {
    '@type': 'Dataset',
    name: 'GISTEMP annual global temperature anomalies, selected 1960–2025',
    description:
      '66 NASA GISTEMP v4 J-D annual anomalies, baseline 1951–1980, retained at source precision for an annotated chart example.',
    creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'NASA GISS' },
    isBasedOn: 'https://data.giss.nasa.gov/gistemp/',
    temporalCoverage: '1960/2025',
    distribution: {
      '@type': 'DataDownload',
      encodingFormat: 'text/csv',
      contentUrl: 'https://www.chartsai.com/editorial/data/temperature.csv',
    },
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Annotated Temperature Chart: NASA Data & Method | ChartsAI"
  description="Make an annotated line chart from NASA GISTEMP annual data. Inspect the baseline, all 66 values, source citation and free editable publication downloads."
  schema={schema}
  imageHeight={900}
  imageAlt="Original source-based publication chart by ChartsAI"
  imagePath="/editorial/assets/line.png"
  dateModified="2026-09-09"
>
  <article class="container editorial-resource">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/editorial-charts/">Editorial charts</a><span>/</span><span>Temperature record</span>
    </nav><h1>A trend needs context.</h1><p>
      An annotated line chart should tell readers what the values measure, what the reference point means and
      which observation a note describes. This example makes those choices explicit using a pinned NASA
      GISTEMP dataset.
    </p><p>
      By ChartsAI / <a href="https://www.supamakers.com/">SupaMakers</a> · Reviewed September 9, 2026. Source retrieved
      September 9, 2026.
    </p>
    <figure class="editorial-figure">
      <img
        src="/editorial/assets/line.svg"
        width="1200"
        height="900"
        alt="Annual global temperature anomalies from 1960 to 2025, with a marker at 2024"
      /><figcaption>
        Annual global land–ocean temperature anomalies. The selected 2024 value is 1.28°C relative to the
        1951–1980 mean. The note is anchored to that observation.
      </figcaption>
    </figure><div class="editorial-links">
      <a class="button secondary small" href="/line-graph-maker/?publication=1#editor"
        >Edit this annotated line chart</a
      >{
        ['svg', 'png', 'pdf', 'csv', 'json', 'html'].map((format) => (
          <a href={`/editorial/assets/line.${format}`} download>
            {format === 'json' ? 'Editable project' : format.toUpperCase()}
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <h2>What does zero mean?</h2><p>
      These are anomalies relative to the 1951–1980 average, not absolute temperatures and not a
      pre-industrial baseline. Zero marks that reference average. The example includes zero on the vertical
      axis so the reference remains visible. Replacing the baseline label without recalculating the values
      would misrepresent the data.
    </p>
    <h2>Keep the note attached to the observation</h2><p>
      The numbered marker refers to the Global series in the 2024 row. It moves with that data coordinate when
      the canvas changes. Its complete text appears below the plot, where it can be read without hover. The
      editor rejects an annotation that refers to a missing row or series; replacing the data clears previous
      annotations explicitly.
    </p><p>
      A useful note identifies a supported observation. It should not invent a cause for a rise or fall. The
      line joins consecutive annual estimates and does not claim to describe daily temperatures. The CSV and
      data table let readers check the point independently of the graphic.
    </p>
    <h2>Selection and calculations</h2><p>
      We selected the J-D annual column for every year from 1960 through 2025: 66 values, with no omissions,
      interpolation or smoothing. We did not average the monthly columns ourselves. The horizontal scale uses
      numeric years, and the labels retain the source’s two decimal places. This pinned example does not
      include incomplete 2026 data or update automatically.
    </p><p>
      NASA’s estimates may be revised. A retrieved snapshot makes this chart reproducible; it does not make
      the values permanently final. This graphic does not include the uncertainty ensemble. Consult the
      primary method and uncertainty publication when making scientific claims from the record.
    </p>
    <h2>Source and scientific citation</h2><p>
      <a href="https://data.giss.nasa.gov/gistemp/"
        >GISTEMP Team (2026), GISS Surface Temperature Analysis, version 4, NASA GISS</a
      >, accessed 2026-09-09. Also cite <a href="https://doi.org/10.1029/2023JD040179"
        >Lenssen et al. (2024), A GISTEMPv4 observational uncertainty ensemble</a
      >. NASA’s GISTEMP page provides citation and image-credit guidance; retain the source acknowledgment
      when reusing this example. ChartsAI created this graphic from the source values; NASA has not endorsed
      the presentation.
    </p><div class="editorial-links">
      <a href="/editorial/data/temperature.csv" download>Selected annual CSV</a><a
        href="/editorial/data/nasa-temperature-source.csv"
        download>Original NASA table</a
      ><a href="/editorial/data/manifest.json" download>Selection and checksums</a>
    </div>
    <details id="data">
      <summary>All 66 source values · anomaly in °C</summary><div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>NASA GISTEMP v4 annual J-D values · baseline 1951–1980</caption><thead
            ><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Global anomaly (°C)</th></tr></thead
          ><tbody
            >{
              data.temperature.slice(1).map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <>
                    <th scope="row">{row[0]}</th>
                    <td>{row[1]}</td>
                  </>
                </tr>
              ))
            }</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
    <h2>Reuse the workflow</h2><p>
      Open the <a href="/line-graph-maker/?publication=1#editor">publication line maker</a>, inspect the
      source example, then import your own table. Keep the unit and reference period close to the headline.
      The <a href="/editorial-charts/publishing-guide/">publishing guide</a> explains SVG, HTML, captions and the
      optional AnimCharts file handoff. For multiple overlapping trends, compare the <a
        href="/small-multiples-chart-maker/">small multiples maker</a
      >.
    </p>
  </article></Layout
>
